## Authentication

The provider must sign in to the server before it can make changes. Choose one of these three
methods:

| Method | Settings | When to use it |
|--------|----------|----------------|
| Basic authentication | `username` and `password` | Use it to get started and in test labs. The user must be an administrator of the tenant. |
| OAuth2 client credentials | `client_id` and `client_secret`, and optionally `scopes` | Use it for automation, such as CI/CD pipelines. The credentials belong to an M2M (machine-to-machine) application that is allowed to call the management APIs. |
| Access token | `access_token` | Use it when your own tooling already gets a token. The provider sends the token as it is and never refreshes it. |

The provider reads credentials from the provider block first. If the block sets any credential,
the provider ignores all credential environment variables. For example, a provider block that sets
`client_id` and `client_secret` ignores `WSO2IS_USERNAME` and `WSO2IS_PASSWORD`.

When you use client credentials, the provider asks for the `SYSTEM` scope by default. The server
replaces `SYSTEM` with every scope that the application is allowed to use. This means you control
what Terraform can do by choosing which APIs the application may call. The
[Authentication guide](guides/authentication) shows how to create this application with Terraform.

## Tenants and organizations

A **tenant** is a separate root organization on the server, with its own users, applications and
settings. Every server starts with the `carbon.super` tenant.

* Set `tenant_domain` to manage a tenant other than `carbon.super`. Users of that tenant sign in
  as `user@tenant-domain`.
* Set `organization_id` to manage a sub-organization (a B2B customer) instead of the root
  organization. The provider gets a token for the sub-organization with the `organization_switch`
  grant and then calls the organization APIs (the paths that contain `/o/`).

Use a separate provider block, with its own `alias`, for each organization. The organization ID can
come from a `wso2is_organization` resource in the same configuration. The
[Organizations guide](guides/organizations) shows a complete example.

## TLS certificates

The provider checks the server's TLS certificate. It trusts the certificate authorities of your
operating system and any certificate you give in `ca_certificate`. You can set `ca_certificate`
to the PEM text of the certificate or to the path of a PEM file.

A new WSO2 Identity Server uses a self-signed certificate that nobody trusts. On a local test
server, you can set `insecure_skip_verify = true` to turn off the check. Do not turn it off for a
server that other people use.

## Environment variables

You can set every provider setting with an environment variable instead. This keeps secrets out
of your configuration files.

| Setting | Environment variable |
|---------|----------------------|
| `base_url` | `WSO2IS_BASE_URL` |
| `tenant_domain` | `WSO2IS_TENANT_DOMAIN` |
| `organization_id` | `WSO2IS_ORGANIZATION_ID` |
| `username` and `password` | `WSO2IS_USERNAME` and `WSO2IS_PASSWORD` |
| `client_id` and `client_secret` | `WSO2IS_CLIENT_ID` and `WSO2IS_CLIENT_SECRET` |
| `scopes` | `WSO2IS_SCOPES` (separate the scopes with spaces) |
| `access_token` | `WSO2IS_ACCESS_TOKEN` |
| `ca_certificate` | `WSO2IS_CA_CERTIFICATE` |
| `insecure_skip_verify` | `WSO2IS_INSECURE_SKIP_VERIFY` |
| `request_timeout` | `WSO2IS_REQUEST_TIMEOUT` |
| `max_retries` | `WSO2IS_MAX_RETRIES` |

## How the resources work

The resources fall into a few groups. Each group behaves a little differently.

**Objects.** Applications, users, roles, identity providers and similar resources are objects.
Terraform creates an object when you add its resource and deletes the object when you remove the
resource. If you leave out an optional attribute, the server uses its own default value. For most
attributes, the provider also reads back the server's value. As a result, a change that someone
makes in the Console shows up as a difference in the next `terraform plan`.

**Tenant-wide settings.** Resources such as the `*_configuration` resources,
`wso2is_governance_connector`, `wso2is_validation_rules`, `wso2is_flow` and the branding resources
manage settings that always exist on the server. When you create one of these resources, Terraform
takes control of the settings that you list. The provider leaves all other settings alone. Destroying
one of these resources either restores the server defaults or only removes the resource from the
Terraform state. Each resource page tells you which one happens.

**Secrets.** Most APIs never send secrets back, such as client secrets and passwords. The provider
keeps the value from your configuration in the state. It cannot notice when someone changes the
secret in the Console. Some resources offer write-only arguments, such as `password_wo` and
`value_wo`, which need Terraform 1.11 or later. A write-only argument keeps the secret out of the
state. To send a new value, change the secret and increase the matching `*_version` number.

**Import.** You can import every resource. The import ID format is at the end of each resource
page. To bring many existing objects under Terraform, add `import` blocks and run
`terraform plan -generate-config-out=generated.tf`. Terraform then writes the configuration for you.

## Retries and parallel changes

The provider retries a request when the server answers with HTTP 429, 502, 503 or 504, or when
the connection fails. Set `max_retries` to change how often it tries again.

The server stores some tenant-wide settings, such as governance connectors and server
configurations, in one shared record. Two changes to that record at the same time can overwrite
each other. To prevent this, the provider makes changes to these settings one at a time. You can
still manage many of these resources in the same configuration.

